Chapter 17 – Part 3 
Curtis locked the phone and tipped his head back against the wall, long black braids falling across the black technical hoodie as a slow breath left him.
“…What do I do, Justice?”
The street did not answer. Neither did the dark above the streetlights. There was only the residual noise of Ikebukuro at evening, the cooling metal of the phone in his hand, and the familiar shape of a question he used to be able to hand to someone else.
If Justice were here, he would already be laughing.
Or telling him he was overcomplicating it.
Or telling him he was undercomplicating it and about to walk face-first into three different kinds of attachment while pretending he was only being helpful.
Curtis closed his eyes for a second.
Celty wanted one clean truth.
Anri was already holding pieces of him carefully.
Rio kept checking whether he made it home.
The van gang had started placing him.
Izaya was still hungry.
And somewhere under all of it was the simple fact that he did not know how to stay soft without becoming a target for other people’s need.
He opened his eyes again and stared at the empty air like it might finally answer.
“…Yeah,” he muttered. “That’s what I thought.”
The phone stayed dark in his hand.
The question stayed unanswered.
And for the first time since arriving in the city, Curtis looked less like a man managing the board and more like a man who had run out of clean moves.
Curtis pushed off the wall and started walking.
The evening crowd thinned the farther he got from the main strip, black technical hoodie blending into the darker side streets, long black braids shifting against his back with each step. After a few minutes he pulled out his phone, not for messages this time, but for the map.
He stopped.
Stared.
Then exhaled through his nose.
“…I should’ve asked to be dropped off closer.”
The little blue dot sat much farther from his neighborhood than his pride wanted to admit. Somewhere between the rescue, the roadside argument, the second van confrontation, and his dramatic exits, he had managed to end the night on the wrong side of a long walk. He locked the screen with a faint scowl and kept going anyway.
He made it another half block before the phone vibrated again.
Celty.
Celty:
You’re taking too long to answer.
That usually means you’re either asleep, avoiding me, or walking somewhere thinking too hard.
A second message followed almost immediately.
Celty:
Which one is it.
Curtis looked down at the screen, then at the long stretch of unfamiliar evening street ahead of him, and let out a quiet, humorless breath.
Of course she could time it like that.
Curtis sighed under the streetlight, long black braids shifting as he muttered to himself,
“…Well. At least she’s consistent.”
He typed back with one thumb while still walking.
Curtis:
I never asked for a ride home from Kadota and co.
I’m out wandering my way home lol
The reply took a few seconds.
Celty:
Of course you are.
A pause.
Celty:
You help with a trafficking extraction, then just… start walking across half the ward like that’s a normal cooldown.
Celty:
Are you far.
Another message followed, more practical than soft:
Celty:
If you get lost, say so.
Ikebukuro folds in on people who think they can out-wander it at night.
Curtis huffed once through his nose, almost amused despite himself, and kept moving. Even through text, she managed to sound like someone who had already decided whether she was annoyed at him or watching out for him — and had not fully chosen which.
Curtis kept walking as he typed, the evening air cool against his face.
Curtis:
I got maps on my phone.
It’s really nice out.
Who knows if the night’s kind to me.
I might not even go home tonight.
Well — until morning. School.
The answer came back faster this time.
Celty:
That’s a very you way of saying you’re avoiding your apartment.
A short pause.
Celty:
Maps don’t make the city safer.
They just make it easier to get lost with confidence.
Celty:
If you’re not going home, at least stay where there are people.
Empty streets get ideas at night.
Then, after a longer beat:
Celty:
…Text me when you decide what you’re actually doing.
I don’t like the version of this where you disappear until morning and call it “nice out.”
Curtis glanced at the message, mismatched eyes half-lidded under the streetlights, and slipped the phone lower in his hand without answering immediately. The night was nice. That much was true.
Whether that made it kind was a different question.
Curtis typed while crossing toward a brighter stretch of street.
Curtis:
I’m technically a tourist too.
So the sights are nice.
Don’t worry about me. I can handle myself.
Something on your mind?
The typing indicator appeared almost at once. Then stopped. Then appeared again.
Celty:
Yes.
A pause.
Celty:
You.
And the Tech Rider.
Celty:
You keep answering around it.
You were with Kadota’s people today.
You move like him.
You timed a message after that race like a joke only one of you would understand.
Another line landed harder than the rest:
Celty:
I’m asking again.
Are you him.
No softening. No room to turn it into weather or wandering. Just the question, waiting under the streetlights with him.
Curtis stopped under the streetlight and sighed at the screen.
He did not answer the question.
Instead he opened a rideshare app, ordered the first available car, and waited with the phone face-down in his palm like that could postpone the rest of the night. By the time the car dropped him outside his building, the earlier edge of the evening had thinned into something quieter and more dangerous: a woman on the other end of the line who was done accepting partial truths.
He unlocked the two-bedroom flat, kicked the door shut behind him, and only then typed.
Curtis:
I’m at home.
If you want to talk.
The reply came almost immediately.
Celty:
Where.
Curtis stared at the single word, then huffed under his breath and typed back.
Curtis:
Woah.
I meant over the phone.
A pause. Then she persisted.
Celty:
I know what you meant.
I asked where.
Celty:
If you’re going to keep splitting yourself into pieces over text, then say so.
If you’re actually willing to talk, stop choosing the version of distance that protects you most.
Celty:
Where do you live, Curtis.
The apartment sat quiet around him. Long black braids fell forward as he looked from the phone to the dark living room and back again, caught between the safety of a screen and a headless woman who had decided that screens were no longer enough.
Curtis stared at the screen a second longer, then sighed and typed.
Curtis:
Sorry.
Sending my address.
He dropped the pin, locked the phone, and did not give himself time to take it back.
Instead of staying inside, he stepped back out into the night. The two-bedroom flat stayed dark behind him while he took up a post near the building entrance, still in the same black technical hoodie and cargo pants from the day, long black braids neat against his chest. He lit a cigarette, the first drag burning slow as he leaned against the wall and watched the street.
He did not have to wait long.
The familiar engine note reached him first — not quite ordinary machinery, never only a motorcycle. Shooter rolled out of the darker end of the block with Celty astride it, yellow helmet turned toward the building, toward the tall figure smoking under the entrance light.
She slowed. Stopped. Let the engine fall to a low idle.
For a moment neither of them moved.
Curtis took one more drag, mismatched eyes steady on the yellow helmet, the cigarette end glowing faintly between his fingers. No sealed Tech Rider visor. No voice changer. No van gang, no extraction lot, no convenient distance of text.
Just him.
Celty sat on Shooter in the night air and looked at the man who had finally stopped choosing every version of distance that protected him most.
Curtis took the cigarette from his mouth just long enough to speak, voice dry and ordinary in the night air.
“Yo.”
Celty remained still on Shooter for a second, yellow helmet fixed on him like she was matching the man in front of her against every other version she had already met — the text thread, the street conversation, the sealed rider from the race, the one who moved too cleanly during the extraction.
Then she shut the engine off.
Her real voice came through the private channel, quieter than the helmet made her look.
“…‘Yo.’”
A short pause.
“You make me ride across the ward for answers, and that’s how you open.”
She swung off Shooter, boots hitting the pavement, and stood facing him fully under the entrance light. The yellow helmet tilted a fraction.
“You’re really just standing out here smoking like this is normal.”
Another beat. Less sharp. More tired underneath.
“I’m here, Curtis. No screen. No exit. Start talking.”
Curtis flicked ash from the cigarette, then motioned with two fingers for her to follow.
He didn’t explain. Just turned and led her down the side path into the building’s parkade. The air changed under concrete — cooler, thicker with oil and old exhaust. Their footsteps echoed between the pillars until he stopped beside a familiar red Softail parked in a clean, deliberate slot.
Celty’s yellow helmet turned toward it at once.
Same lines.
Same machine.
The one that had taken an impossible gap the hard way and kept running with a complaint in its frame.
She said nothing, but the private channel went very still.
Curtis continued upstairs.
His flat was nicer than most people would have expected from the way he wandered the city — clean, paid for, lived-in without being cluttered. He left Celty in the main room long enough to disappear into the bedroom. Drawers. A closet. The soft sound of gear being moved. When he came back out, he didn’t put the sealed helmet on. He only stood there in the black technical hoodie and cargo pants from the day, long black braids falling against his chest, and met the yellow helmet head-on.
“I’m the same person.”
The words were simple. No theatrics. No apology for the delay.
Celty remained near the entrance of the living room, boots planted, Shooter’s absence from the room somehow still leaving her looking armed. When her real voice finally came through the private channel, it was low and tightly controlled.
“…Of course you are.”
A short, sharp pause.
“The race. The recovery. The timing of that text. The way you moved during the extraction.” Her helmet tilted slightly. “I kept trying to leave room for coincidence. You didn’t leave me any.”
Another beat. Heavier.
“So this is you. All of it. The man on the phone. The one who hears me. And the rider with the glowing visor.”
She took one step farther into the flat, not aggressive, but done being held at a distance.
“Then stop making me ask in pieces. If you’re the same person… start acting like one.”
Curtis stayed where he was in the middle of the flat, long black braids resting against the black technical hoodie, mismatched eyes steady on the yellow helmet.
“Well. What do you want to know?”
Celty didn’t answer immediately. She stood in the living room like someone measuring the distance between every version of him she had already met, the private channel quiet for a full breath before her real voice came through again.
“Start with the obvious.”
A short pause.
“Why hide it. Why race me sealed and then text me after like it was a private joke. Why help Kadota’s people as yourself and still leave enough room for me to think there might be two of you.”
Her helmet tilted slightly.
“And don’t give me the tourist answer. Or the ‘I can handle myself’ answer. If you were willing to send your address and bring me up here, then you’re willing to give me the real version.”
Another beat, quieter but no less firm.
“I want to know what I’m actually standing in front of, Curtis. Not the convenient pieces.”
Curtis exhaled through his nose and rubbed the back of his neck once, long black braids shifting with the motion.
“I brought you here because you sounded uncomfortable when talking about Mei.” His voice stayed even, stripped of the earlier distance. “I raced you last night to help you take a load off. You seemed like you had fun. I wasn’t hiding it. I just got caught up in the van gang’s business, and there was no time to tell you while we were saving his friend.”
The explanation settled into the flat between them — practical, almost ordinary, and completely insufficient for how tangled the last two days had felt from her side.
Celty stood silent for a moment, yellow helmet unreadable, the private channel holding nothing but the faint edge of her breathing.
Then her real voice came through, quieter than anger and sharper than relief.
“…So all of that was you trying to be considerate.”
A short pause.
“You raced me in a sealed helmet to make me feel better. You timed a text after like a breadcrumb. You spent half the day with Kadota’s people, moved like the same rider I couldn’t place, and then decided the right moment to explain was after I’d already started splitting you into separate men in my head.”
Her gloved hands flexed once at her sides.
“I’m not saying the reasons are ugly. They’re not. That’s the problem.” Another beat. “You’re standing there telling me every contradiction was kindness. And maybe it was. But from my side, it felt like being handled by someone who keeps choosing when I’m allowed to know which version of him I’m talking to.”
The yellow helmet lifted a fraction.
“You don’t get to call that ‘not hiding’ just because your intentions were good, Curtis.”
Curtis looked down for a second, long black braids falling forward over his shoulders.
“Every version is me.” His voice had gone quieter. “But if you came for the version no one gets to see… me, at my most vulnerable point.”
He reached down, gripped the hem of the black technical hoodie, and pulled it off in one motion with the shirt beneath it.
The living room light left nothing polite about what it revealed.
His torso was a map of survival more than anatomy. Old slash marks crossed denser muscle in pale ridges. Animal tears. Bullet scars sunk deep enough to have rewritten the flesh around them. Torture work with deliberate spacing. Across his back and over the shoulders, the longer raised lines of a bullwhip sat like a language carved there by someone who had wanted the lesson to last. None of it looked theatrical. It looked lived-in. Paid for.
Curtis stood there bare to the waist, mismatched eyes steady on the yellow helmet, and kept talking as if the display itself were part of the apology.
“I get that only getting pieces of me is frustrating. And what I did earlier, without saying anything sooner, came off cruel. I really didn’t mean for that, Celty. I meant to tell you much earlier in the day. But I got caught up in all this stuff today.” A short pause. “So to make it up… hopefully seeing a piece no one ever gets to see helps. Every version you saw — if you piece me all together — it’s just me.”
The flat went very quiet.
Celty did not move at first. The yellow helmet remained fixed on the scarred landscape of him, on the evidence that the blunt, capable man who raced her, texted her, and pulled traffickers apart had been assembled out of years no clean story would cover.
When her real voice finally came through the private channel, it was stripped of most of its earlier anger. What remained was heavier.
“…Put like that, it becomes harder to stay furious at you.”
A longer silence stretched between them.
“You’re right about one thing. That is not a version most people get.” Her gloved hands flexed once at her sides, then stilled. “Shinra has cut people open for less history than what you’re wearing on your skin. And you’re just… standing there with it. Offering it like proof of honesty.”
Another beat. Softer. Still unsettled.
“It does help. Not because scars make a man trustworthy by default. Because you’re finally not managing the distance.” The yellow helmet lifted from his chest back to his face. “But don’t mistake this for the end of the conversation, Curtis. Showing me what the world did to you isn’t the same as explaining why you keep deciding how much of yourself other people are allowed to hold at once.”
She took one slow step closer.
“I’m still here. That should tell you something. Now keep talking.”
Celty’s real voice had already answered him.
What she did not say stayed locked under the yellow helmet, private even from the channel.
The first thing she registered was not desire. It was inventory. Years of living around Shinra had trained her eye for damage, and Curtis’s body was a catalogue of it — layered, repeated, survived. Slash over slash. Animal marks. Bullet entries that had closed wrong. The deliberate geometry of torture. The bullwhip lines across the back that made her think, unpleasantly, of systems designed not just to hurt but to teach ownership. She had seen modified flesh before. She had been modified flesh before. That should have made this clinical.
It did not.
Because under the scars, the body itself was hard to treat as only evidence. Broad through the shoulders. Dense through the chest and abdomen. The kind of build that did not come from vanity gym work so much as necessity repeated until it became structure. Even standing still, he carried force in the lines of him. The scars did not make him look ruined. They made the strength underneath look expensive.
And she was still a woman under the legend and the helmet and the years of distance.
That part arrived late and unwelcome, but it arrived: the heat of awareness, the brief, involuntary recognition of living muscle and proximity, of a man who had stripped down not to perform but to stop managing what she was allowed to see. It complicated the anger. It complicated the sympathy. It made the room feel smaller than it had a minute ago.
She hated that it mattered at all.
Because the more honest reaction sat underneath even that — not he is attractive, not simply he is damaged, but the quieter, more dangerous thought:
He survives like someone who never expected to be held carefully afterward.
And standing in his flat, looking at the proof of that, Celty found herself less certain which part unsettled her more: the history written into his skin, the body that carried it, or the fact that he had trusted her with both.
Curtis let the silence sit only a second longer before shifting back into something more ordinary.
“Okay. So what do you want to talk about?”
He didn’t wait for the answer immediately. He turned and disappeared into the bedroom again, the door left half-open, fabric sounds brief and practical. When he came back out, the scars were covered once more by a fitted black long-sleeve compression shirt and simple dark joggers, long black braids falling neat against his chest. The softer home version of him — still solid, still scarred underneath, but no longer intentionally exposed.
He tugged once at the hem of the shirt, almost self-conscious.
“Sorry. Having my shirt off too long feels alienating unless I’m showering.”
Celty watched him reappear like that, the yellow helmet angling slightly as the rawer version of him vanished back under black fabric. The private channel stayed quiet for a beat before her real voice returned, less sharp than before.
“…That makes sense.”
A short pause.
“You don’t have to leave it off for my benefit. The point wasn’t the display. It was that you stopped managing the distance long enough to show me something real.” Her helmet tilted toward the now-covered line of his shoulders. “Covering it again doesn’t undo that.”
Another quieter beat.
“As for what I want to talk about…” She remained near the living room’s open space, boots planted, presence still too deliberate for casual company. “You. The pieces that are still only half-named. Why kindness is the first tool you reach for, and distance is the second. And why a man built like that, with a history like that, keeps acting surprised when people don’t stay neutral around him.”
The yellow helmet faced him fully.
“Sit. If we’re doing this without screens, we’re doing it properly.”
Curtis sat on the couch, long black braids falling forward over the fitted black compression shirt, and looked at her directly.
“Then please take off the helmet.”
Celty was still for a second. Then she reached up, unfastened the yellow helmet, and lifted it away.
Where a head should have been, there was only the clean termination of a pale neck and a constant rise of black shadow-smoke, curling and dispersing into the air of the flat like breath with no mouth to shape it. The rest of her remained as the city usually never got to see without armor and myth in the way — black riding suit formed of living shadow, gloves, boots, the unmistakable outline of a woman who had learned to move through the human world without ever fully belonging to it.
She set the helmet on the table and sat across from him.
Her real voice came through the private channel, quieter in the room without the yellow shell to frame it.
“There. No screen. No helmet. No convenient version of me either.” A short pause, smoke drifting upward from the empty space above her collar. “You asked what I wanted to talk about. Then start with this: why does someone who keeps surviving the worst versions of people still offer the softest parts of himself so easily… and then act surprised when those soft parts become attachment points?”
The black smoke rose between them in slow ribbons as she waited.

Elsewhere, in Shinra’s apartment, the conversation had turned more interesting than Mei expected.
Shinra sat forward with the bright, clinical enthusiasm he reserved for rare specimens and complicated patients, hands loosely folded.
“Shizu-chan brought a boy over the other day,” he said. “Foreign. Tall. Braids. Heterochromia. Took a board to the head and two stab wounds from Izaya and still walked into my place talking about duct tape like a reasonable medical plan.”
Mei arched a brow, pink hair shifting as she listened.
“And?”
“And his body was full of old scars. Not decorative. Functional history. Animal. Blade. Human. Other.” Shinra’s glasses caught the light as he tipped his head. “Rh-null blood, too. Extremely rare. He talked about Appalachia like it was an ordinary childhood setting, and about bleeding out in forests like other people talk about bad commutes. Fascinating restraint, considering what he was carrying.”
Mei’s smile thinned with interest.
“So Shizuo dragged home another impossible person.”
“Essentially.” Shinra looked almost fond of the memory. “He didn’t like being stared at. Told me not to say a word about the scars while I stitched him. Very firm about boundaries. Very unconcerned about pain.”
He did not mention anything he had not been present for. No van gang. No race. No private texts. No sealed helmet in the night. Only the boy who had sat in his apartment bleeding, sharp-tongued, and incompletely explained.
Mei rested her chin in one hand.
“…I’d like to meet him.”
Shinra smiled.
“Something tells me Ikebukuro isn’t finished throwing him into other people’s living rooms.”
Curtis sat forward on the couch, long black braids falling over the black compression shirt as he chose the words carefully.
“That’s a complicated thing for me to answer without giving more history of myself. But…” He met the rising black smoke where her face would have been. “I trust you, Celty. Please don’t make me regret it.”
A short pause. The apartment felt smaller around the admission.
“Before, I wouldn’t ever look twice at helping someone. Figured they could pull themselves out. I didn’t care. But Justice did. Since we were kids, we poked our noses in other people’s business. Often I was too cold and blunt. Justice would often say for me to be more considerate of other people’s feelings.”
He shook his head once.
“It wasn’t until we both got deported that we saw injustices even worse. Suddenly being abused and abandoned didn’t seem so bad.” Another heavier beat. “Then we both got captured by the cartel and tortured for weeks. Then some things happened — parts I’m not ready to talk about — but we wiped out an entire cartel line. Or rather, I did. Justice didn’t want me to. But the psychological damage had been done.”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes lowered briefly, then rose again.
“I never cared when I was tortured. But kids. Teens. Young women. Men protecting their families. Those monsters didn’t care.”
Black smoke continued to rise from the empty space above Celty’s collar. For a long moment she did not move. When her real voice finally came through the private channel, it was stripped of challenge and left with something closer to careful gravity.
“…That’s why you move toward the taken ones.”
Not a question. A placement.
“You didn’t become kind because the world was gentle to you. You became kind because you watched what happens when nobody intervenes — and because Justice kept dragging your conscience into the open until it grew teeth of its own.” A short pause. The smoke drifted sideways in the still air. “And the part you won’t name yet… that’s the part that still costs you.”
Her gloved hands rested against her knees.
“I won’t make you regret trusting me with this. Not by running it to Shinra. Not by turning it into curiosity.” Another quieter beat. “But I understand better now. Why distance and softness live in you at the same time. Why you can look at other people’s suffering and refuse to call it none of your business… even when closeness itself still makes you want to leave the room.”
The black smoke rose between them like a held breath.
“Thank you for telling me. Even the incomplete version.”
Curtis’s mouth tightened.
“You don’t want to know the details of what I did to those people. That’s why I left it out.”
He sighed, and something in the sound was wrong — not dramatic, just thinned, like air leaving a room that had suddenly remembered older weather. For a second his mismatched eyes lost focus, the present flattening under whatever had risen up behind it. He stood too carefully, long black braids shifting against the compression shirt as he crossed into the kitchen and started making coffee with mechanical precision. Water. Grounds. The click of the machine.
“Sorry about everything….”
When he said it, his eyes were pale in a way that had nothing to do with color. He was still facing the counter, still moving, but only partly in the apartment with her. The rest of him looked stranded somewhere between a forest, a border, and a room where screaming had once been ordinary.
Celty rose a fraction in her seat, black smoke curling sharper above her collar as she tracked the change.
Her real voice came through the private channel low and immediate.
“Curtis.”
Not sharp. Anchoring.
“You’re not there anymore.” A short pause as she watched his hands continue the coffee routine without him fully inhabiting them. “Whatever just came back can stay unfinished. You don’t owe me the details, and you don’t have to stand in that room again just because you opened the door a little.”
The smoke drifted toward him in the quiet.
“Come back to the counter. To the flat. To now.” Another beat, steadier. “I’m still here. You’re not alone in that memory unless you leave me outside it on purpose.”
She remained where she was, helmet off, no demand in her posture — only the clear refusal to let him disappear into the pale middle distance without someone calling him out of it.
Curtis blinked a few times.
Color returned to his mismatched eyes in slow degrees, the pale distance draining out of them as the kitchen, the coffee machine, and the headless woman on his couch reasserted themselves as the real room. He shook his head once and forced a small smile that did not fully reach the rest of his face.
“I’m fine.”
He came back to the couch with the coffee, sat down, and wrapped both hands around the cup like the heat could finish the job of bringing him all the way back. Long black braids fell forward as he looked into the dark surface of the drink instead of at her.
“Sorry about that. My mind still goes to those places.” A short pause. The smile was already gone. “When Justice died… that’s when it started.”
Black smoke rose quietly from the empty space above Celty’s collar. She did not accept I’m fine at face value, but she also did not corner him for the full anatomy of the fracture. When her real voice came through the private channel, it was low and measured.
“…Of course it did.”
A beat.
“You lost the person who used to pull you back. The one who told you to be more considerate. The one who was there for the deportation, the capture, the aftermath.” Her gloved hands rested still against her knees. “It makes sense that the worst rooms in your head got louder after that. It also makes sense that you say you’re fine before you actually are.”
The smoke drifted sideways in the quiet flat.
“You don’t have to dress it up for me. If those places still open on their own, then they still open. Sitting here drinking coffee doesn’t make that untrue.” Another softer pause. “But thank you for coming back when I called. That matters more than the apology.”
She inclined the empty line of her neck slightly toward him.
“I’m not asking you to relive his death tonight. Only… don’t insult both of us by pretending it left nothing behind.”